{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the birth of facebook with precision, tension, and emotional depth. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is not just a biopic—it’s a chilling exploration of ambition, betrayal, friendship, and the cost of innovation in the digital age. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached, a man driven more by a need to prove himself than by a desire to connect with others. his performance is understated yet electric, making zuckerberg both sympathetic and alienating.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally strong. andrew garfield shines as justin timberlake’s portrayal of erik schmidt, the loyal friend turned bitter rival, while jesse plemons brings quiet intensity to the role of the twins who feel betrayed by their own creation. timberlake, as sean parker, is magnetic and chaotic, embodying the reckless energy of the early tech boom—charismatic, dangerous, and intoxicating.\n\nsorkin’s script is a tour de force: rapid-fire dialogue that crackles with intelligence and wit, weaving together multiple legal narratives with seamless efficiency. the courtroom scenes, framed as parallel depositions, serve as a narrative backbone, revealing the story in fragments that slowly coalesce into a devastating portrait of how relationships fracture under the weight of money and power. the pacing is relentless, and every line feels deliberate, every pause loaded.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is immaculate. the visual style is cool, sleek, and precise, mirroring the sterile, algorithmic world of social media. the use of light, shadow, and composition creates a sense of isolation even in crowded rooms. trent reznor and atticus ross’s score is haunting and minimalist, a digital heartbeat 